    "text.ca1aa5a5": null,
    "text.ca2de674": null,
    "text.ca6075b5": "Few (少) and many (多) refer to whether the number set aside in one round is small or large: 4 and 5 are small (few), 8 and 9 are large (many). The traditional method reads few as yang and many as yin. iYeok sees this pairing, too, not as a fixed answer but as a standard each person can choose, and plans to let you set it in the options. The details are in Learn's [Why did this line come out?].",
    "text.ca6e0e92": "Kui · Opposition",
    "text.cabe9209": null,
    "text.cac3f3d3": null,
    "text.cb5bcac1": null,
    "text.cb751a1b": null,
    "text.cc09715f": null,
    "text.cc2e3af8": null,
    "text.cce62a08": null,
    "text.cd5a6294": null,
    "text.cd6e6b6f": "We see yin and yang not as something divided by the body's sex, but as qualities that live in everyone. What we often call masculinity and femininity also live together in one person, and can show themselves in different ways through time, experience, and our own choices.",
    "text.cd95c748": "Huan · Dispersion",
    "text.cde876b1": "Could not load the hexagram data. Please check your connection and reload.",
